import json
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
talks = []
items = []
problems = []

def talk(record,date,speaker,pages,title,kind,summary):
    talks.append(dict(record=record,date=date,speaker=speaker,pages=pages,title=title,kind=kind,summary=summary))

def item(record,pages,kind,title,notes,statement,reconstruction,cls,fate,status,
         literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",verification="read the result",
         development="annotated",problem="",worth="",refs=(),confidence="high",area="",speaker=None):
    n = sum(x["record"] == record for x in items) + 1
    d = dict(id=f"1981-{record}.{n}",record=record,pages=pages,kind=kind,title=title,
             notes=notes,statement=statement,reconstruction=reconstruction,**{"class":cls},
             fate=fate,present_status=status,literal_correctness=literal,publication_match=match,
             verification=verification,development=development,problem=problem,worth=worth,
             refs=list(refs),confidence=confidence,area=area)
    if speaker: d["speaker"] = speaker
    items.append(d)

def problem(pid,title,statement,occurrences,status,refs=()):
    problems.append(dict(id=pid,title=title,statement=statement,occurrences=occurrences,status=status,
                         status_date="2026-11",refs=list(refs)))

def save():
    for name,data in [("talks",talks),("items",items),("problems",problems)]:
        (ROOT/f"{name}.json").write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+"\n")

talk(545,"","","P001001-P001002","Year 1982","heading",
     r"The catalog's undated year heading marks the transition to calendar year 1982 within the academic year 1981--82. It supplies no independent mathematical assertion. Its catalog page range overlaps Müller's dated lecture and must not create a duplicate talk or duplicate occurrence.")
talk(546,"1982-02-08","Werner Müller","P001001-P001005","Lattice gravity and curvature of piecewise flat spaces","talk",
     r"Müller reports joint work with Jeff Cheeger and Robert Schrader. A formal path-integral motivation leads to Regge's hinge curvature, nondegenerate smooth triangulations, convergence of curvature measures, higher Lipschitz--Killing curvatures, finite-dimensional metric spaces of simplices, and an integral-geometric proof idea. Gelfand asks which curvature actions have lower-order field equations. The source explicitly separates curvature convergence from the still unclear convergence of the equations. The catalog range extends through P001007; the next dated heading actually starts at P001006. Several faithful mathematical errors require emendation, especially the heat sign, the Euler-form trace, and the edge-length Jacobian.")
talk(547,"1982-02-15","V. L. Ginzburg [catalog attribution]","P001006","Hamiltonian actions and the moment map","talk",
     r"A short announcement records the definition of a Hamiltonian Lie-group action and its moment map, including infinitesimal equivariance and Poisson compatibility. The manuscript heading says only Ginzburg; the initials are those of the supplied catalog and are not independently established by the page. No additional theorem or unpublished program is recoverable. This content ends before the dated Arnold heading on P001007.")
talk(549,"1982-02-22","V. I. Arnold","P001007","Integral geometry and lattice analogues","talk",
     r"Arnold compares the parallel-body polynomial, curvature integrals, projection averages, and Euler-characteristic Crofton formulas, then proposes their lattice analogues and refers to McMullen's Vancouver congress lecture. The lattice discussion states normalized-volume and quotient-lattice ideas, not a fully quantified discrete replacement for every continuous formula. This heading precedes Gelfand's Macdonald-book intervention on the physical page, although the catalog numbers put record 548 first.")
talk(548,"1982-02-22","I. M. Gelfand","P001007-P001008","A partition complement identity and Guillemin's symplectic cones","talk",
     r"Gelfand gives a combinatorial exercise from Macdonald's book and its generating-function corollary, then introduces symplectic cones from work of Guillemin on Weyl asymptotics. The partition identity is identifiable exactly; its written corollary omits a conjugation and an inverse argument. The cone discussion introduces the subject resumed under Arnold's heading in record 550. The surviving notes state no final new spectral asymptotic theorem.")
talk(550,"1982-02-22","V. I. Arnold","P001008-P001009","Contact cones, Weyl quantization and axiomatic operator algebras","talk",
     r"Arnold's intervention identifies the contact quotient of a symplectic cone. The ensuing exposition gives a Weyl kernel and axioms for complex-order operator classes: symbolic composition, asymptotic completeness, boundedness, compactness, trace-class thresholds, detection of principal symbols, splitting, and holomorphic order families. The connection with Guillemin's later axiomatic Weyl-algebra paper is identifiable, but the original 1985 article was not available in full during this audit. Local uniqueness, global quantization, and spectral conclusions must be kept separate.")
talk(552,"1982-03-01","Robert MacPherson","P001010","Proposed topics: perverse sheaves and bivariant theories","plan",
     r"The handwritten English explicitly introduces titles of a paper he would speak about. One diagram joins perverse sheaves, holonomic differential modules, and characteristic varieties; another proposes a theory attached to maps with homology and cohomology as its endpoints. These are substantive identifiable announcements, but the page does not document a completed lecture or proofs. The second topic matches the already published Fulton--MacPherson bivariant theory.")
talk(553,"1982-03-01","Dusa McDuff","P001010-P001011","Canonical classes of volume-preserving diffeomorphism groups","talk",
     r"McDuff constructs the homotopy fiber of the discrete-to-topological comparison, the universal transverse volume foliation, and its compactly supported cohomology classes. She records a torus test, the parity of the top class for spheres, and the opposite parity of a secondary class on Euclidean space. These claims match her 1983 paper, whose author bibliography reproduces its abstract; the full proofs were not obtained. Fuchs's interjection occurs inside this discussion and has its own catalog record.")
talk(551,"1982-03-01","D. B. Fuchs","P001010","A subgroup-fibration analogy during McDuff's talk","other",
     r"Fuchs contributes the familiar subgroup fibration and a heuristic about a very dense subgroup having an infinitesimal fundamental domain. The homotopy fiber is the mathematically reliable object here; the naive quotient for the identity map from a group with discrete topology cannot serve as its replacement. This is an intervention, not a separate developed lecture. Its source position follows the beginning of McDuff's record 553.")
talk(554,"1982-03-01","John Milnor","P001011-P001012","Homology of Lie groups made discrete","talk",
     r"Milnor asks how the homology of a Lie group changes after forgetting its topology. The mod-prime isomorphism conjecture is accompanied by rational vanishing for compact groups, a large integral homology obstruction, solvable cases, finite-coefficient split surjectivity, integral cohomological injectivity, and low-dimensional results. The 1983 article was read and provides exact theorem-level identifications. Its proved partial statements do not settle the general Friedlander--Milnor conjecture.")
talk(555,"1982-03-01","André Haefliger","P001012-P001013","Smooth coinvariants for the modular action on the circle","talk",
     r"Haefliger asks whether every smooth function on the projective circle is a sum of the two generator coboundaries for the modular group. Exact smooth solvability is called unknown; smooth density is asserted separately. The source links the question to foliations with minimal leaves but gives no theorem making the connection precise. The companion proves exact continuous solvability, independently checked, while leaving smooth regularity unresolved. Kirillov's next heading is on P001014, outside this record.")

# Record 546: the source's numerical labels do not coincide with occurrence numbers.
item(546,"P001001","construction","Schrödinger evolution and the formal Feynman kernel",
 r"For \(H(p,q)=p^2/(2m)+V(q)\), the page sends \(q\) to multiplication and \(p\) to \(+i\hbar\partial_x\), writes evolution \(e^{-it\widehat H/\hbar}\), and calls its position kernel a transition probability. Its formal path expression has exponent \(-iS\), with \(S=\int(\dot x^2-V)\,dt\).",
 r"For real bounded \(V\), one elementary rigorous setting is the self-adjoint operator \(\widehat H=-(\hbar^2/2m)\partial_x^2+V\) on \(H^2(\mathbb R)\), whose unitary evolution solves \(i\hbar\partial_t f=\widehat Hf\). The kernel is an amplitude (generally a distribution), not a probability density. The conventional action is \(\int(m\dot x^2/2-V)\,dt\), with formal phase \(e^{iS/\hbar}\). A product of Lebesgue measures over all times is not a defined countably additive path-space measure.",
 r"The self-adjoint domain and bounded-potential hypothesis are a sufficient modern reconstruction, not the most general theorem. Changing the momentum sign alone leaves this quadratic Hamiltonian unchanged; the displayed phase and omitted action constants require separate correction.","E",
 r"This is the pre-existing path-integral motivation, not the new result of the talk. The book cited in the notebook is Konopleva--Popov; a separately verified historical reference is \citet[book and errata information]{feynmanhibbs1965path}. No claim of a rigorous real-time measure is attributed to that citation.",
 r"As of November 2026, unitary Schrödinger evolution is standard. Equations (2), (4), and (5) are not mutually normalized as written; the formal path integral must remain explicitly formal.",
 literal="false as written",match="thematic only",verification="publisher metadata checked",refs=["feynmanhibbs1965path"],area="Mathematical physics")
item(546,"P001002","theorem","The heat-semigroup interpretation of the path integral",
 r"The notes credit Kac with giving meaning to the integral after \(i\mapsto1\). They write \(\hbar\partial_t f=+\widehat Hf\), followed by \(e^{-t\widehat H/\hbar}\), and put \(x(\tau)\), rather than the potential, inside the time integral in the exponential.",
 r"For \(m,\hbar>0\) and real bounded continuous \(V\), Brownian motion with generator \((\hbar/2m)\partial_x^2\) gives \((e^{-t\widehat H/\hbar}f)(x)=\mathbb E_x[e^{-\hbar^{-1}\int_0^t V(B_s)ds}f(B_t)]\). This solves \(\hbar\partial_t f=-\widehat Hf\). A fixed-endpoint kernel uses Brownian-bridge expectation multiplied by the free heat kernel; unconditioned Wiener measure is not by itself an endpoint kernel.",
 r"The displayed bounded continuous hypothesis avoids the stronger integrability assumptions needed for singular potentials. The missing minus sign, missing \(V\), and endpoint normalization are emendations of a faithfully copied formula.","E",
 r"Kac's 1949 article is the historical source identified in the notebook; its original DOI was attempted but full access failed in this run, so that entry is marked unverified rather than presented as a theorem read in the original. The formula above is checked directly against the heat generator and is a standard reconstruction, not a new claim by Müller.",
 r"As of November 2026, the Feynman--Kac formula is established. The real-time-to-imaginary-time slogan does not by itself justify analytic continuation or a gravitational measure.",
 literal="false as written",match="exact",verification="indirect",refs=["kac1949distributions"],area="Probability and mathematical physics")
item(546,"P001002","theorem","The Einstein--Hilbert action has second-order field equations",
 r"The scalar-curvature action contains second derivatives of the metric, so fourth-order equations seem possible; a local noncovariant divergence term is said to remove them.",
 r"On a closed smooth manifold, the first variation of \(\int_M\operatorname{Scal}_g\,d\operatorname{vol}_g\) with respect to an inverse-metric variation has interior coefficient \(\operatorname{Ric}_g-\tfrac12\operatorname{Scal}_g g\). The remaining terms are divergences. The resulting vacuum equation is second order in \(g\); adding a volume term adds a multiple of \(g\). On a manifold with boundary one must specify boundary conditions or the appropriate boundary action.",
 r"Closedness or boundary conditions are not stated. The local first-derivative expression need not be globally covariant, whereas its Euler--Lagrange tensor is.","E",
 r"This is the classical Einstein--Hilbert variational identity. It is also the first nonconstant member of the natural-tensor family classified by Lovelock; see the precise modern formulation in \citet[Theorem 2.6]{navarronavarro2011lovelock}.",
 r"As of November 2026, this order reduction is established. The manuscript's historical aside about what Einstein initially believed is not independently verified and is not used as mathematical evidence.",
 refs=["navarronavarro2011lovelock"],area="Differential geometry and gravitation")
item(546,"P001002","question","Which curvature actions have lower-order Euler--Lagrange equations?",
 r"Gelfand asks \ru{Найти все комб. кривизн, где получ. ур-е меньшего порядка}; the pencilled answer is Einstein and Pontryagin classes.",
 r"A precise established version fixes dimension and metric signature and asks for all natural symmetric divergence-free tensors depending on the metric through its two-jet. Their real vector space is spanned by the Lovelock tensors \(L_0,\ldots,L_{\lfloor(n-1)/2\rfloor}\). The associated Lovelock scalar densities yield second-order metric equations. In their top dimension Euler and Pontryagin characteristic numbers have identically zero interior variation; they are null Lagrangians, not additional nonzero field equations.",
 r"The notebook neither fixes dimension nor defines lower order, polynomiality, covariance, or equivalence modulo divergences. The natural-tensor statement is a carefully bounded reconstruction. It is not a classification of every conceivable curvature-dependent action. In dimensions at least five, the nontrivial Gauss--Bonnet Lovelock tensor already shows why an unqualified Einstein-only nontrivial answer is incomplete.","E",
 r"The relevant classification predates the seminar: Lovelock's original paper appeared in 1971. Its exact tensor theorem and a proof were read in \citet[Theorem 2.6]{navarronavarro2011lovelock}, first circulated in May 2010 and published in 2011. The 1971 publisher page was not fully accessible and is separately marked unverified.",
 r"As of November 2026, the reconstructed natural second-order tensor problem is solved. The unrestricted wording of the notebook cannot be assigned that theorem without the stated restrictions, and its proposed answer needs amendment.",
 literal="false as written",match="partial",problem="GP-1981-20",refs=["lovelock1971einstein","navarronavarro2011lovelock"],area="Differential geometry and gravitation",speaker="I. M. Gelfand")
item(546,"P001002,P001004-P001005","method","A formal gravitational integral and finite triangulation measures",
 r"The notes integrate \(e^{-S[g]-\kappa\operatorname{Vol}(g)}\) over metrics modulo diffeomorphisms and formally write \(\prod_x(\det g(x))^{5/2}\prod_{\mu\leq\nu}dg_{\mu\nu}(x)\). They later propose finite-dimensional measures on compatible simplex metrics.",
 r"For a fixed finite triangulation \(P\), admissible edge lengths form an open subset of a finite-dimensional real vector space. A specified smooth Riemannian metric on that subset defines an ordinary volume measure. A continuum integral on \(\operatorname{Met}(M)/\operatorname{Diff}(M)\) additionally requires a measure, gauge treatment, integrability, and a limiting prescription, none of which follows from the notation \(\prod_xdg(x)\). For covariant positive-definite \(n\times n\) matrices the congruence-invariant density is \((\det g)^{-(n+1)/2}\prod_{i\leq j}dg_{ij}\), so the four-dimensional exponent is negative.",
 r"The finite-dimensional construction is recoverable; a continuum existence theorem is not stated. The sign diagnosis assumes the covariant matrix entries actually written, not a silent change to contravariant variables.","F",
 r"The finite Regge program is identifiable in the 1981 symposium announcement published as \citet{cheegermullerschrader1982lattice}. Neither that bibliographic match nor the curvature theorem of \citet{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature} certifies the formal continuum measure in (9)--(10).",
 r"As of November 2026, no exact continuum-measure theorem is identifiable from this fragment. This F classification concerns the unquantified formal integral, not the well-defined simplex measures indexed separately.",
 literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",verification="indirect",refs=["cheegermullerschrader1982lattice","cheegermullerschrader1984curvature"],confidence="medium",area="Mathematical physics")
item(546,"P001003","definition","Piecewise flat metrics and Regge hinge curvature",
 r"A metric is encoded by positive edge lengths \(\ell:P^{(1)}\to\mathbb R_+\). At a codimension-two simplex the angle defect is \(2\pi\) minus the sum of incident dihedral angles, and \(R(P)\) is the defect times hinge volume summed over hinges.",
 r"For a finite triangulated closed \(n\)-manifold with compatible nondegenerate Euclidean simplex metrics, define \(\delta_h=2\pi-\sum_{\sigma\supset h}\theta_{h,\sigma}\) and \(\mathcal R(P)=\sum_h\delta_h\operatorname{vol}_{n-2}(h)\). Positive edge lengths must satisfy the simplex Gram positivity conditions, not merely positivity. With the convention \(\operatorname{Scal}=2K\) on surfaces, the scalar-curvature integral corresponding to this hinge sum has factor \(2\mathcal R(P)\); other normalizations absorb this factor.",
 r"Closedness excludes boundary hinges, whose defect convention differs. The notes call \(\mathcal R\) scalar curvature without fixing the factor of two; this is made explicit rather than silently identifying inequivalent conventions.","E",
 r"Regge's original paper appeared in 1961, long before the talk; the publisher record \citet{regge1961relativity} verifies its title, pages and chronology. The hinge construction is the starting point of \citet[Introduction]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature}.",
 r"As of November 2026, the construction is established. It defines a curvature measure concentrated on hinges, not a smooth pointwise curvature tensor.",
 match="exact",refs=["regge1961relativity","cheegermullerschrader1984curvature"],area="Discrete differential geometry")
item(546,"P001003","theorem","Convergence of Regge curvature under nondegenerate refinement",
 r"After \(\operatorname{mesh}(P_j)\to0\), the notes announce convergence of total Regge curvature to the smooth integral, then add that angles must stay bounded away from zero and a smooth triangulation is used.",
 r"Let \((M,g)\) be a closed smooth Riemannian manifold and let secant piecewise flat approximations arise from admissible smooth triangulations with mesh tending to zero and a uniform positive fatness bound. The corresponding Lipschitz--Killing curvature measures converge weakly to the smooth ones; in particular their total scalar-curvature terms converge with consistent normalization. Uniform fatness controls every simplex face and excludes slivers; mesh convergence alone is insufficient.",
 r"The detailed approximation and fatness hypotheses are supplied from the published theorem, not all from the shorthand angle condition. Normalizations must agree with the preceding item. No convergence of derivatives of the action is inferred.","A",
 r"The seminar is a documented 1982 circulation point, after the 1981 Heisenberg Symposium announcement (published 1982). The full result was read in \citet[Theorem 5.1, pp. 427--428]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature}, published in 1984.",
 r"As of November 2026, this is an established curvature-measure convergence theorem. It does not establish convergence of critical points of the discrete actions.",
 match="exact",refs=["cheegermullerschrader1982lattice","cheegermullerschrader1984curvature"],area="Discrete differential geometry")
item(546,"P001003","question","Does curvature convergence imply convergence of the Regge equations?",
 r"Immediately after the curvature theorem the page says \ru{Пока неясно, сходятся ли ур-я} (it is still unclear whether the equations converge).",
 r"For a specified family of fat triangulations approximating a smooth metric, one must distinguish convergence of the Regge action, convergence of its first variation or Einstein tensor in a distributional norm, and convergence of solutions of the resulting discrete boundary-value problem. The strongest literal reading asks about the equations; it supplies no gauge, boundary conditions, topology of solution convergence, or uniqueness class. A precise bounded subproblem is convergence of the distributional Einstein tensor of Regge interpolants of a fixed smooth metric.",
 r"The three-way distinction and the interpolant subproblem are editorial reconstructions. The question is not promoted to an unqualified conjecture that arbitrary stationary Regge metrics converge to a prescribed Einstein metric.","C",
 r"For the subproblem, \citet[Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.3, pp. 16--17 of the preprint]{gawlikneunteufel2023einstein} prove convergence in an \(H^{-2}\) norm of the distributional densitized Einstein tensor for suitable positive-degree Regge finite-element interpolants on shape-regular meshes, in dimension at least three. The preprint first appeared in October 2023 and the journal version online in March 2025 (2026 volume). This is a partial match, not a theorem about convergence of all discrete solutions; degree-zero interpolation is not covered by the same positive conclusion.",
 r"As of November 2026, distributional tensor approximation has rigorous positive results under explicit mesh and degree hypotheses. The notes' unspecified convergence-of-equations question, and especially convergence of actual stationary metrics under a definite gauge and boundary problem, is not resolved by the sources verified here.",
 literal="not determinable",match="partial",verification="read the result",problem="GP-1981-21",worth=r"Worth a further narrowly specified investigation: an explicit stable boundary-value problem could separate consistency of the discrete curvature from convergence of its solutions.",refs=["cheegermullerschrader1984curvature","gawlikneunteufel2023einstein"],confidence="medium",area="Geometric numerical analysis")
item(546,"P001003","method","Fat smooth triangulations and the danger of repeated barycentric subdivision",
 r"The notes discourage barycentric subdivision because angles become small, illustrate a better planar subdivision, and refer to Müller's Ray--Singer torsion work for smooth triangulations.",
 r"A useful approximation family must combine mesh tending to zero with scale-invariant lower bounds on simplex shape. One standard fatness condition bounds \(\operatorname{vol}_j(\tau)/\operatorname{diam}(\tau)^j\) away from zero for every \(j\)-face \(\tau\). Repeated refinement must be chosen to preserve such bounds. The planar midpoint refinement illustrates shape preservation for a triangle; it is not by itself a construction in all dimensions.",
 r"The explicit all-face fatness definition makes the notebook's angle requirement precise. The torsion paper is a bibliographic pointer, not an assertion that every smooth triangulation or every barycentric subdivision sequence works.","E",
 r"The geometric condition is defined and used in \citet[Section 1]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature}. Müller's pre-talk torsion article was published in 1978; its title and date were checked in his academy bibliography, while no exact lemma in it is claimed here.",
 r"As of November 2026, nondegeneracy remains an essential hypothesis. The translation unnecessary weakens the Russian practical warning not to use such a refinement when it destroys the angle bound.",
 match="partial",refs=["cheegermullerschrader1984curvature","muller1978torsion"],area="Discrete differential geometry")
item(546,"P001003-P001004","construction","Higher piecewise flat curvature from flags and solid angles",
 r"Formula (18) sums hinge volumes in codimension \(2k\), multiplied by alternating products of normalized angles along nested even-codimension face flags. The margin says norm.; the next page normalizes full solid angle to one.",
 r"A piecewise flat manifold carries intrinsic Lipschitz--Killing curvature measures in even curvature degrees. Their local weights are universal alternating angle expressions attached to face flags, multiplied by the corresponding face volumes. Once the empty flag, angular normalization, and universal dimensional constants are fixed, these give the discrete counterparts of the smooth Lipschitz--Killing densities.",
 r"The manuscript does not state all flag ranges or normalization constants. Its displayed sum is retained as evidence, while the exact published definition is the reference for a usable formula; arbitrary constants are not reconstructed from the margin.","A",
 r"The matching detailed definition is in \citet[Sections 2--3]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature}, following the jointly announced lattice-gravity program of 1981--82. This is the curvature family actually treated there, not an arbitrary collection of Pontryagin forms.",
 r"As of November 2026, the construction is established. The notebook's abbreviated flag sum is a partial transcription of the definition, insufficient for numerical use without the missing conventions.",
 match="partial",refs=["cheegermullerschrader1984curvature"],area="Discrete differential geometry")
item(546,"P001004","theorem","Higher curvature convergence and the Euler-form correction",
 r"The notes write \(R_k(P_j)\to\int_M\operatorname{tr}(\Omega^k)\) and, for \(k=n/2\), identify that integral with \(\chi(M)\). They later mention multiplication by a standard form.",
 r"The convergent quantities are consistently normalized Lipschitz--Killing curvature measures, with smooth densities formed by the appropriate alternating curvature contractions and coframes. In even dimension \(n=2m\), the top density is the Euler form \(\operatorname{Pf}(\Omega/2\pi)\), with the standard orientation convention, whose integral is \(\chi(M)\). It is not generally \(\operatorname{tr}(\Omega^m)\). A trace of a matrix-valued curvature power has degree \(2m\) but represents a different invariant polynomial; in dimension two the orthogonal curvature matrix has trace zero.",
 r"The replacement of the trace by the Lipschitz--Killing density is substantive mathematical emendation, not a corrected reading of the handwriting. The dimension-two countercheck is our elementary verification of the mismatch.","A",
 r"\citet[Introduction and Theorem 5.1]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature} explicitly identify the top Lipschitz--Killing measure with Euler characteristic and establish curvature-measure convergence. The paper does not support the trace notation literally.",
 r"As of November 2026, the corrected theorem is established; the literal Euler trace identity is false, even for a round two-sphere.",
 literal="false as written",match="partial",refs=["cheegermullerschrader1984curvature"],area="Characteristic classes and discrete geometry")
item(546,"P001004","theorem","An angle-flag Gauss--Bonnet formula for Euler characteristic",
 r"Formula (20) expresses \(\chi(M)\) as an alternating sum of products of normalized solid angles along flags starting at vertices and passing through even-dimensional faces.",
 r"For a closed even-dimensional piecewise flat manifold, the total normalized top Lipschitz--Killing curvature equals its Euler characteristic. A flag-angle expression computes the vertex weights. The empty-chain contribution and the convention for normalized full solid angle are necessary for the formula to hold, including in dimension zero.",
 r"The topological conclusion is identifiable; the exact unprinted summation conventions are not. This item separates Gauss--Bonnet from the erroneous matrix-trace expression preceding it.","E",
 r"Combinatorial and polyhedral Gauss--Bonnet formulas predate the talk. \citet[Section 3]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature} supply the particular intrinsic angle framework used in this announcement.",
 r"As of November 2026, the normalized identity is established. The notebook's bare summation should not be used without the empty-flag convention.",
 refs=["cheegermullerschrader1984curvature"],area="Characteristic classes and discrete geometry")
