 r"The strongest literal interpretation is that \(\operatorname{ran}D\) is dense in the usual Fr\'echet topology of \(C^\infty(\mathbb {RP}^1)\). By continuous-dual separation, this is equivalent to absence of a nonzero distribution invariant under both \(S\) and \(T\). Even if this assertion holds, a dense range in a Fr\'echet space need not be the whole space. Absence of invariant finite measures only addresses order-zero distributions and does not prove the stated smooth density.",
 r"The topology is read literally from the source's \(C^\infty\), not silently weakened to the uniform norm. The continuous theorem proved in this companion implies uniform density of smooth coboundary sums by smooth approximation of continuous solutions, but it does not establish density in every derivative seminorm.","C",
 r"No exact independently verified reference or proof for the notebook's smooth-density assertion was obtained. The invariant-functional terminology in \citet[abstract]{abouqatebelkacimi2000invariant} is thematic only. The development identifies the missing distinction and reduces exact smooth solvability to surjectivity of the averaged difference operator \(\tfrac12(I+C_S)(I-C_T)\) onto the \(S\)-invariant smooth functions.",
 r"As of November 2026, smooth density is retained as an assertion in the notes, not promoted to a proved result of this companion. Exact \(C^0\) solvability and the elementary averaging reduction are proved; the higher-order distribution and regularity questions remain outside those proofs.",
