problem("GP-1981-20","Curvature actions with lower-order field equations",
 r"Gelfand asks for curvature combinations whose metric Euler--Lagrange equations have lower order. A bounded precise version classifies natural symmetric divergence-free metric two-tensors of differential order at most two in fixed dimension and signature, modulo the separate issue of null Lagrangians. The unrestricted source wording is broader and lacks a specified class of actions.",
 ["1981-546.4"],
 r"As of November 2026, the reconstructed natural-tensor problem is solved by Lovelock's pre-1982 classification; \citet[Theorem 2.6]{navarronavarro2011lovelock} gives a verified exact formulation. The handwritten Einstein-and-Pontryagin answer is not a complete all-dimensional classification. Earlier Einstein-action discussions are thematic antecedents, not this definite classification question.",
 ["lovelock1971einstein","navarronavarro2011lovelock"])
problem("GP-1981-21","Convergence of the equations of Regge gravity",
 r"For nondegenerate piecewise flat approximations of a smooth metric, determine a precise topology and geometric hypotheses under which the first variations or discrete Einstein equations converge. This must be separated from convergence of the curvature action and from convergence of actual solutions with fixed gauge and boundary data; the source specifies neither of the latter.",
 ["1981-546.8"],
 r"As of November 2026, \citet[Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.3 of the preprint]{gawlikneunteufel2023einstein} establish a rigorous positive-degree interpolant result for the distributional densitized Einstein tensor in \(H^{-2}\). This is a partial resolution of a specified consistency problem. No general convergence theorem for arbitrary stationary Regge metrics was verified. Curvature-measure convergence alone does not imply convergence of variational equations.",
 ["cheegermullerschrader1984curvature","gawlikneunteufel2023einstein"])
problem("GP-1981-22","The Friedlander--Milnor comparison with finite coefficients",
 r"For every finite-dimensional Lie group \(G\), prime \(p\), and degree \(q\geq0\), is \(H_q(BG^\delta;\mathbb F_p)\to H_q(BG;\mathbb F_p)\) an isomorphism? All coefficients are constant, and the topology on \(G\) on the right is its Lie topology. The corresponding statement for every finite abelian coefficient group is equivalent.",
 ["1981-554.2"],
 r"As of November 2026, retain C: solvable identity components, finite-component split surjectivity, and specified low-degree cases are verified in \citet{milnor1983discrete}; stable developments are recorded in \citet{friedlander2008conjecture}. The renewed 2021 preprint claim \citep{amrani2015friedlander} was located but not certified by an independent proof check. The 2025 publisher extract \citep{dranishnikov2025generalized} still labels the general Lie statement a conjecture. No accepted general resolution was verified in this audit.",
 ["milnor1983discrete","friedlander2008conjecture","amrani2015friedlander","dranishnikov2025generalized"])
